## Reasoning

**Distinct entities in the schema.** The source is a timeseries of ADS-B state vectors. Each row is one broadcast sample from one aircraft at one instant. So the real-world entities are:

1. **The aircraft itself** — identified by `icao24`, the 24-bit ICAO transponder address, which is stable across records ("ab58b2", "3f7a21"). This is the only column that identifies a persistent real-world object; `callsign` varies per flight and `squawk` is shared by several aircraft in the sample (7777 appears twice), so neither can be the subject.
2. **The sample/observation itself** — the `time` column is the timestamp of the report, and the ontology models this as its own class one hop from Vehicle: `bsm:Observation` with `bsm:observationDateTime`, reached by `bsm:hasObservation` (declared on `bsm:Vehicle`, and `bsm:Aircraft` is a Vehicle). This is exactly the "linked class whose columns sit on the same row" case, so it becomes its own subject rather than a literal on the aircraft.
3. **The position** — `lat`/`lon`. `bsm:BattlespaceConcept.hasGeographicLocation` leads to `bsm:Location`, which in turn carries `bsm:hasGeometry` to a `bsm:Geometry`; the concrete subclass with `bsm:latitudeDecimal`/`bsm:longitudeDecimal` is `bsm:AbsolutePoint`. So two nested entities: a Location wrapper and an AbsolutePoint.
4. **The velocity vector** — `velocity` (m/s) and `vertrate` map to `bsm:Velocity` (`bsm:speedMetersPerSecond`, `bsm:inclinationAngle`), reached by the unattached `bsm:hasVelocity`.
5. **The orientation** — `heading` maps to `bsm:Orientation.headingAngle`, reached by `bsm:hasOrientation`.
6. **Altitudes** — `bsm:Altitude` (EXT) with `bsm:altitudeMeters`, reached by `bsm:hasAltitude`. There are two independent altitude measurements per row (barometric and geometric), and only one altitude property exists, so each becomes its own Altitude instance under the same object property.

**Subject expressions.** The aircraft is `aircraft_{icao24}` — one node per airframe, deliberately merging its many samples. Every per-sample entity must be keyed by both aircraft and time, otherwise successive samples would collapse into one node: `observation_{icao24}_{time}`, `location_{icao24}_{time}`, `point_{icao24}_{time}`, `velocity_{icao24}_{time}`, `orientation_{icao24}_{time}`, `altitude_baro_{icao24}_{time}`, `altitude_geo_{icao24}_{time}`. `icao24` and `time` are populated in every record of a timeseries, so nothing is dropped.

**Near-synonym check on `heading`.** The ontology offers `bsm:absoluteBearingAngle` ("course over ground / track angle") on Velocity and `bsm:headingAngle` ("direction the longitudinal axis points, degrees true") on Orientation. The qualifier that decides is *track over ground* vs *airframe axis*. The column is called plainly `heading` and gives no indication of which, so this is genuinely ambiguous; I follow the literal column name to `bsm:headingAngle` but record the ambiguity and reduced confidence rather than defaulting to the more common ADS-B reading (track angle).

**Near-synonym check on `vertrate`.** The only vertical component of `bsm:Velocity` is `bsm:inclinationAngle`, expressed in degrees, while `vertrate` is a rate (m/s, values -2.5 / 0.0 / 4.8). Same physical axis, mismatched quantity kind — I map it but flag low confidence on the unit disagreement.

**`squawk`.** `bsm:AlternateIdentifier` is the bundle-one-value-with-its-scheme shape the guidance warns about. Here, though, the entity genuinely carries identifiers under distinct standards at once — the ICAO 24-bit address (`icao24`), the flight callsign (`callsign`), and the Mode 3/A transponder code (`squawk`) — so the schema does exhibit multiple naming schemes for the same aircraft. I attach `squawk` as a structured AlternateIdentifier with an explicit standard-code constant, at moderate confidence. Its subject is keyed by aircraft plus squawk value, since a squawk can be reassigned.

**`callsign`** goes to `bsm:alternateName`, whose description explicitly names "callsigns, transponder-broadcast names". `icao24` serves both as `bsm:conceptName` (MIM-mandatory primary name) and `bsm:sourceIdentifier` (traceability back to the source record key).

**Unmapped.** `onground` is a boolean airborne/on-surface flag; the nearest candidate, `bsm:isActiveIndicator`, means "in service" for Materiel, which is a different assertion, so I leave it unmapped rather than force it.

**Final IRI check.** Each IRI value's transformation was copied from the referenced entity's subject_transformation string: `location_{icao24}_{time}`, `point_{icao24}_{time}`, `velocity_{icao24}_{time}`, `orientation_{icao24}_{time}`, `altitude_baro_{icao24}_{time}`, `altitude_geo_{icao24}_{time}`, `observation_{icao24}_{time}`, `altid_squawk_{icao24}_{squawk}` — all match character for character. No empty transformation objects are emitted; the key is omitted wherever the raw value is used as-is.

